<spell-notebook "zoom usage">
	<header level="1">
		Zoom
		<header>A zoom shows something small in the page, and full size over it on a click.</>
	</>
	<zoom-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#plain-screenshots">Plain screenshots</>
				<ui-item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-zoom>` shows something small in the page, and full size over it.

			- Round any content:  the content is laid out at its full size, then drawn at `shrink` of it (`50%` by default).
			- `max-width` and `max-height` are the most room it takes in the page, as CSS lengths;  past them it's cut off.
			- A click on it, or Enter, shows the content full size in a modal, at the width it had in the page.
			  The page keeps its room meanwhile, so nothing below it moves.
			- `open` says it's showing;  set it to open it from script.
			- With `src` it shows that image full size instead, its `alt` the caption:  open while `src` is set.

			`<ui-image zoom>` opens its image the same way, with no wrapper.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<ui-zoom max-width="320px" max-height="200px" shrink="40%">
			  <ui-table>...</ui-table>
			</ui-zoom>
			<ui-image zoom src="shots/toolbar.png" alt="The toolbar"></ui-image>
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Plain screenshots">
		<markdown>
			A page can open every plain `<img>` screenshot full size, with none of them rewritten:  `ImageZoom` listens on the
			whole page, and makes the page's one `<ui-zoom>` the first time.
			The epic pages, guides and details pages do it.

			- It opens an image shown at least 64px, and smaller than its own size, in a `<figure>`, or in a link to an image.
			- Never an icon, an image inside another link or a button, inside a `<ui-zoom>`, or anything marked `data-no-zoom`.
			- The pointer over one marks it `data-ui-zoom`:  give it the zoom-in cursor.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			import { ImageZoom } from "spell-ui/components/ui-zoom/ImageZoom"

			document.addEventListener("click", ImageZoom.onClick)
			document.addEventListener("pointerover", ImageZoom.onPointerOver, { passive: true })
			// and in the page's CSS:  img[data-ui-zoom] { cursor: zoom-in }
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			- Tab reaches the zoom's button;  Enter or Space shows it full size.
			- Escape shuts it, and focus goes back where it was.
			- `<ui-image zoom>` is a button too, unless it's a `link`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The content in the page is `inert` while it's shrunk:  a reader meets it once, full size.
			  One button over it, named "Show full size", opens it.
			- Full size, it's in a modal dialog, which keeps focus inside until it's shut.
			- The zoomed image's own name is its alt text, then ", full size";  without one, "Image, full size".
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
